Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?feat(kanban):=20default=20placement=20=E2=80=94?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20a=20card=20with=20no=20local-only=20need=20is=20created=20on?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20the=20routed=20gateway=20[daveey]?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On 2026-10-27 the MacBook (16 cores) ran 11-14 default-board Kanban workers at once and its load
average peaked at 430; David's rule is that automation runs on hermes-hub. Of 455 MacBook cards
sampled over 7 days (classified by the tool calls their workers made), ~21% touched nothing local
and ~25% needed hard MacBook-only access (AWS SSO profiles, Chrome, Keychain, Apple apps/TCC).

- kanban.placement.route maps this gateway's hermes-hub name to a target ({"macbook": "hub"}).
  kanban_create then creates a card with no local-only need on the target through the origin-bound
  'hermes-hub create-task <gw> --origin-session <chat> --creator-session ...' path, so it still
  shows in the chat that asked for it. Returns placed{gateway, request_id, task_id once applied}.
- Stays local: placement='local'; parents, project, dir/worktree workspace, non-default board or
  assignee, courier creation, options hermes-hub cannot carry (model/provider, goal_mode, triage,
  initial_status, human_request, non-local completion contract); a requires capability the target
  lacks; a local marker in title/body/skills (Chrome, Keychain, TCC, launchd, Desktop, MacBook, the
  mini, Baby Bear, AWS SSO profiles...); the target not live (stale sync or capability record).
- hermes-hub refusal falls back to a local card; an offline hub is durably queued (rc 3), never
  duplicated. Request ids are stable per (gateway, idempotency key | title/body/origin).
- Remote bodies carry skills and the runtime budget as text, plus how to route a MacBook-only wall
  back with needs=[...] (capability routing).
- Unset route = upstream behaviour.
